\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Admissible fields of one flux are connected}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On an oriented two-dimensional torus with both periods at least #{3}, give each scalar link unit modulus. Fix #{0<\theta\le\pi}. Any two fields of the same scalar \vocabk{plaquette flux}{fdip-015V} with every principal angle of absolute value less than #{\theta} are joined by a continuous link path satisfying the same bound. A representative has all angles #{2\pi w/(N_1N_2)} for flux #{w}.}

\proof{\p{Multiplying all plaquette phases cancels each link against its inverse, giving #{1}. The angle sum is therefore #{2\pi w} for an integer #{w}. Its average lies in the open angle window, and \ref{fdip-015W} realizes that average on every plaquette. The difference between the angle cochains of two fields of flux #{w} has sum zero. On a spanning tree of the dual grid remove a leaf at each step. Give its crossed primal edge a real increment whose oriented contribution is the requested residual at the leaf. The edge contributes its opposite to the neighboring face; update that residual and continue. The final residual vanishes because the sum was zero. The resulting edge cochain #{\alpha} has exactly the prescribed coboundary. Multiplying the first links by #{\ue^{\ui t\alpha}} interpolates the real angles linearly, within the bound.}

\p{The quotient between the endpoint and the desired field now has every plaquette equal to #{1}. Record its two holonomies around the based coordinate cycles. A scalar phase on each link crossing one chosen seam in its positive direction realizes the corresponding holonomy while keeping every plaquette flat. After removing both seam fields, the residual flat field has trivial holonomy along both cycles. Products along grid paths from a base vertex are then independent of the path: interchanging successive perpendicular steps uses a plain unit plaquette, and complete windings have unit product. These site products give a gauge of the trivial field. Vary both seam phases and the finitely many site gauge phases along circle arcs from #{1}. Their product supplies the remaining flat path.}}
